1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:20,240
I think we have to do something about a culture, a piece of the culture of medicine that has

2
00:00:20,240 --> 00:00:24,520
to change, and I think it starts with one physician, and that's me, and maybe I've been

3
00:00:24,520 --> 00:00:28,520
around long enough that I can afford to give away some of my false prestige to be able

4
00:00:28,520 --> 00:00:30,720
to do that.

5
00:00:30,720 --> 00:00:34,760
Before I actually begin the meat of my talk, let's begin with a bit of baseball.

6
00:00:34,760 --> 00:00:35,760
Hey, why not?

7
00:00:35,760 --> 00:00:36,760
We're near the end.

8
00:00:36,760 --> 00:00:39,040
We're getting close to the World Series, right?

9
00:00:39,040 --> 00:00:44,120
We all love baseball, don't we?

10
00:00:44,120 --> 00:00:49,600
Baseball is filled with some amazing statistics, and there's hundreds of them.

11
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:54,120
Moneyball is about to come out, and it's all about statistics and using statistics to build

12
00:00:54,120 --> 00:00:55,120
a great baseball team.

13
00:00:55,120 --> 00:01:00,000
I'm going to focus on one stat that I hope a lot of you have heard of.

14
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:02,280
It's called batting average.

15
00:01:02,280 --> 00:01:05,360
So we talk about a batter who bats 300.

16
00:01:05,360 --> 00:01:12,280
That means that ball player batted safely, hit safely three times out of ten at bats.

17
00:01:12,280 --> 00:01:16,320
That means hit the ball into the outfield, it drops, it didn't get caught, and whoever

18
00:01:16,320 --> 00:01:20,520
tried to throw to first base didn't get there in time, and the runner was safe.

19
00:01:20,520 --> 00:01:23,560
Three times out of ten.

20
00:01:24,480 --> 00:01:29,440
Do you know what they call a 300 hitter in Major League Baseball?

21
00:01:29,440 --> 00:01:31,520
Good.

22
00:01:31,520 --> 00:01:32,520
Really good.

23
00:01:32,520 --> 00:01:34,520
You know, maybe an all-star.

24
00:01:34,520 --> 00:01:38,920
Do you know what they call a 400 baseball hitter?

25
00:01:38,920 --> 00:01:43,680
That's somebody who hit, by the way, four times safely out of every ten.

26
00:01:43,680 --> 00:01:46,380
Legendary.

27
00:01:46,380 --> 00:01:50,960
As in Ted Williams' legendary, the last Major League Baseball player to hit over 400 during

28
00:01:50,960 --> 00:01:51,960
a regular season.

29
00:01:52,960 --> 00:01:56,560
Now let's take this back into my world of medicine where I'm a lot more comfortable,

30
00:01:56,560 --> 00:02:02,360
or perhaps a bit less comfortable, after what I'm going to talk to you about.

31
00:02:02,360 --> 00:02:07,120
Suppose you have appendicitis, and you're referred to a surgeon who's batting 400 on

32
00:02:07,120 --> 00:02:11,680
appendectomies.

33
00:02:11,680 --> 00:02:13,960
Somehow this isn't working out, is it?

34
00:02:13,960 --> 00:02:20,040
Now suppose you live in a certain part of a certain remote place, and you have a loved

35
00:02:20,040 --> 00:02:26,200
one who has blockages in two coronary arteries, and your family doctor refers that loved one

36
00:02:26,200 --> 00:02:29,880
to a cardiologist who is batting 200 on angioplasties.

37
00:02:29,880 --> 00:02:33,560
But, but, you know what, she's doing a lot better this year.

38
00:02:33,560 --> 00:02:38,640
She's on the comeback trail, and she's hitting 257.

39
00:02:38,640 --> 00:02:42,360
Somehow this isn't working, but I'm going to ask you a question.

40
00:02:42,360 --> 00:02:46,760
What do you think a batting average for a cardiac surgeon or a nurse practitioner or

41
00:02:46,760 --> 00:02:53,360
an orthopedic surgeon, an OBGYN, a paramedic, is supposed to be?

42
00:02:53,360 --> 00:02:54,360
One thousand.

43
00:02:54,360 --> 00:02:55,360
One thousand.

44
00:02:55,360 --> 00:02:56,360
Very good.

45
00:02:56,360 --> 00:02:57,360
Okay.

46
00:02:57,360 --> 00:03:02,400
Now, the truth of the matter is, nobody knows in all of medicine what a good surgeon or

47
00:03:02,400 --> 00:03:06,040
physician or paramedic is supposed to bat.

48
00:03:06,040 --> 00:03:09,960
What we do, though, is we send each one of them, including myself, out into the world

49
00:03:09,960 --> 00:03:11,720
with the admonition, be perfect.

50
00:03:11,720 --> 00:03:16,240
Never, ever, ever make a mistake, but you worry about the details, about how that's

51
00:03:16,240 --> 00:03:17,600
going to happen.

52
00:03:17,600 --> 00:03:20,760
And that was the message that I absorbed when I was in med school.

53
00:03:20,760 --> 00:03:24,060
I was an obsessive-compulsive student.

54
00:03:24,060 --> 00:03:32,160
In high school, a classmate once said that Brian Goldman would study for a blood test.

55
00:03:32,160 --> 00:03:34,440
And so I did.

56
00:03:34,440 --> 00:03:38,240
I studied in my little garret at the nurse's residence at Toronto General Hospital not

57
00:03:38,240 --> 00:03:41,760
far from here, and I memorized everything.

58
00:03:41,760 --> 00:03:46,480
I memorized in my anatomy class the origins and insertions of every muscle, every branch

59
00:03:46,480 --> 00:03:52,180
of every artery that came off the aorta, differential diagnoses, obscure and common.

60
00:03:52,180 --> 00:03:56,880
I even knew the differential diagnosis and how to classify renal tubular acidosis.

61
00:03:56,880 --> 00:04:00,120
And all the while, I was amassing more and more knowledge.

62
00:04:00,120 --> 00:04:01,120
And I did well.

63
00:04:01,120 --> 00:04:04,080
I graduated with honors, cum laude.

64
00:04:04,080 --> 00:04:09,800
And I came out of medical school with the impression that if I memorized everything

65
00:04:09,800 --> 00:04:14,920
and knew everything or as much as possible, as close to everything as possible, that it

66
00:04:14,920 --> 00:04:18,460
would immunize me against making mistakes.

67
00:04:18,460 --> 00:04:25,840
And it worked for a while until I met Mrs. Drucker.

68
00:04:25,840 --> 00:04:29,560
I was a resident at a teaching hospital here in Toronto, and Mrs. Drucker was brought to

69
00:04:29,560 --> 00:04:32,560
the emergency department at the hospital where I was working.

70
00:04:32,560 --> 00:04:37,560
At the time, I was assigned to the cardiology service, cardiology rotation.

71
00:04:37,560 --> 00:04:42,000
And it was my job when the emergency staff called for a cardiology consult to see that

72
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:45,940
patient in emerge and to report back to my attending.

73
00:04:45,940 --> 00:04:50,400
And I saw Mrs. Drucker, and she was breathless, and when I listened to her, she was making

74
00:04:50,400 --> 00:04:55,220
a wheezy sound, and when I listened to her chest with a stethoscope, I could hear crackly

75
00:04:55,220 --> 00:04:59,560
sounds on both sides that told me that she was in congestive heart failure.

76
00:04:59,560 --> 00:05:03,160
This is a condition in which the heart fails, and instead of being able to pump all the

77
00:05:03,160 --> 00:05:06,040
blood forward, some of the blood backs up into the lungs.

78
00:05:06,040 --> 00:05:09,560
The lungs fill up with blood, and that's why you have shortness of breath.

79
00:05:09,560 --> 00:05:11,360
And that wasn't a difficult diagnosis to make.

80
00:05:11,360 --> 00:05:14,360
I made it, and I set to work treating her.

81
00:05:14,360 --> 00:05:15,480
I gave her aspirin.

82
00:05:15,480 --> 00:05:17,760
I gave her medications to relieve the strain on her heart.

83
00:05:17,760 --> 00:05:23,000
I gave her medications that we call diuretics, water pills, to get her to pee out the excess

84
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:24,300
fluid.

85
00:05:24,300 --> 00:05:28,440
And over the course of the next hour and a half or two, she started to feel better, and

86
00:05:28,440 --> 00:05:30,720
I felt really good.

87
00:05:30,720 --> 00:05:34,520
And that's when I made my first mistake.

88
00:05:34,520 --> 00:05:36,840
I sent her home.

89
00:05:36,840 --> 00:05:39,060
Actually I made two more mistakes.

90
00:05:39,060 --> 00:05:43,120
I sent her home without speaking to my attending.

91
00:05:43,120 --> 00:05:47,000
I didn't pick up the phone and do what I was supposed to do, which was to call my attending

92
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:51,160
and run the story by him so he would have a chance to see her for himself.

93
00:05:51,160 --> 00:05:52,160
And he knew her.

94
00:05:52,160 --> 00:05:56,400
He would have been able to furnish additional information about her.

95
00:05:56,400 --> 00:05:58,520
Maybe I did it for a good reason.

96
00:05:58,520 --> 00:06:00,860
Maybe I didn't want to be a high maintenance resident.

97
00:06:00,860 --> 00:06:06,380
Maybe I wanted to be so successful and so able to take responsibility that I would do

98
00:06:06,380 --> 00:06:09,780
so and I would be able to take care of my attending's patients without even having to

99
00:06:09,780 --> 00:06:11,980
contact him.

100
00:06:11,980 --> 00:06:15,400
The second mistake that I made was worse.

101
00:06:15,400 --> 00:06:19,860
In sending her home, I disregarded a little voice deep down inside that was trying to

102
00:06:19,860 --> 00:06:22,740
tell me, Goldman, not a good idea.

103
00:06:22,740 --> 00:06:24,320
Don't do this.

104
00:06:24,320 --> 00:06:29,460
In fact, so lacking in confidence was I that I actually asked the nurse who was looking

105
00:06:29,460 --> 00:06:34,140
after Mrs. Drucker, do you think it's okay if she goes home?

106
00:06:34,140 --> 00:06:38,400
And the nurse thought about it and said, very matter of factly, yeah, I think she'll do okay.

107
00:06:38,400 --> 00:06:41,100
I can remember that like it was yesterday.

108
00:06:41,100 --> 00:06:44,420
And so I signed the discharge papers and an ambulance came.

109
00:06:44,420 --> 00:06:46,220
Paramedics came to take her home.

110
00:06:46,220 --> 00:06:49,540
And I went back to my work on the wards.

111
00:06:49,540 --> 00:06:55,000
All the rest of that day, that afternoon, I had this kind of gnawing feeling inside

112
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:56,620
my stomach.

113
00:06:56,620 --> 00:07:00,780
But I carried on with my work and at the end of the day, I packed up to leave the hospital

114
00:07:00,780 --> 00:07:05,620
and walk to the parking lot to take my car and drive home.

115
00:07:05,620 --> 00:07:10,460
When I did something that I don't usually do, I walked through the emergency department

116
00:07:10,460 --> 00:07:12,460
on my way home.

117
00:07:12,460 --> 00:07:16,660
And it was there that another nurse, not the nurse who was looking after Mrs. Drucker before,

118
00:07:16,660 --> 00:07:23,120
but another nurse said three words to me that are the three words that most emergency physicians

119
00:07:23,120 --> 00:07:25,040
I know dread.

120
00:07:25,040 --> 00:07:28,800
Others in medicine dread them as well, but there's something particular about emergency

121
00:07:28,800 --> 00:07:32,800
medicine because we see patients so fleetingly.

122
00:07:32,800 --> 00:07:39,680
The three words are do, you, remember.

123
00:07:39,680 --> 00:07:42,220
Do you remember that patient you sent home?

124
00:07:42,220 --> 00:07:45,160
The other nurse asked matter of factly.

125
00:07:45,160 --> 00:07:48,680
She's back in just that tone of voice.

126
00:07:48,680 --> 00:07:50,120
She was back all right.

127
00:07:50,120 --> 00:07:53,840
She was back in near death.

128
00:07:53,840 --> 00:07:58,520
About an hour after she had arrived home, after I'd sent her home, she collapsed and

129
00:07:58,520 --> 00:08:02,640
her family called 911 and the paramedics brought her back to the emergency department where

130
00:08:02,640 --> 00:08:07,600
she had a blood pressure of 50, which is in severe shock and she was barely breathing

131
00:08:07,600 --> 00:08:08,960
and she was blue.

132
00:08:08,960 --> 00:08:13,400
And the emergency staff pulled out all the stops, they gave her medications to raise

133
00:08:13,400 --> 00:08:17,360
her blood pressure, they put her on a ventilator.

134
00:08:17,360 --> 00:08:24,400
And I was shocked and shaken to the core and I went through this roller coaster because

135
00:08:24,400 --> 00:08:28,480
after they stabilized her, she went to the intensive care unit and I hoped against hope

136
00:08:28,480 --> 00:08:30,560
that she would recover.

137
00:08:30,560 --> 00:08:34,200
And over the next two or three days, it was clear that she was never going to wake up.

138
00:08:34,200 --> 00:08:37,920
She had irreversible brain damage.

139
00:08:37,920 --> 00:08:42,400
And the family gathered and over the course of the next eight or nine days, they resigned

140
00:08:42,400 --> 00:08:47,000
themselves to what was happening at about the nine day mark they let her go.

141
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:54,600
Mrs. Drucker, a wife, a mother, and a grandmother.

142
00:08:54,600 --> 00:08:59,160
They say you never forget the names of those who die, and that was my first time to be

143
00:08:59,160 --> 00:09:01,600
acquainted with that.

144
00:09:01,600 --> 00:09:08,600
Over the next few weeks, I beat myself up and I experienced for the first time the unhealthy

145
00:09:08,600 --> 00:09:15,300
shame that exists in our culture of medicine, where I felt alone, isolated, not feeling

146
00:09:15,300 --> 00:09:19,260
the healthy kind of shame that you feel because you can't talk about it with your colleagues.

147
00:09:19,260 --> 00:09:23,420
You know that healthy kind when you betray a secret that a best friend made you promise

148
00:09:23,420 --> 00:09:29,060
never to reveal and you get busted and then your best friend confronts you and you have

149
00:09:29,060 --> 00:09:32,940
terrible discussions, but at the end of it all, that sick feeling guides you and you

150
00:09:32,940 --> 00:09:36,820
say, you know, I'll never make that mistake again and you make amends and you never make

151
00:09:36,820 --> 00:09:38,220
that mistake again.

152
00:09:38,220 --> 00:09:41,620
That's the kind of shame that is a teacher.

153
00:09:41,620 --> 00:09:45,380
The unhealthy shame I'm talking about is the one that makes you so sick inside.

154
00:09:45,380 --> 00:09:53,020
It is the one that says, not that what you did was bad, but that you were bad.

155
00:09:53,020 --> 00:09:57,220
And it was what I was feeling and, you know, it wasn't because of my attending.

156
00:09:57,220 --> 00:09:58,220
He was a doll.

157
00:09:58,220 --> 00:10:01,500
He talked to the family and I'm quite sure he smoothed things over and made sure that

158
00:10:01,500 --> 00:10:04,300
I didn't get sued.

159
00:10:04,300 --> 00:10:06,940
And I kept asking myself these questions.

160
00:10:06,940 --> 00:10:08,940
Why didn't I ask my attending?

161
00:10:08,940 --> 00:10:10,420
Why did I send her home?

162
00:10:10,420 --> 00:10:15,220
And then at my worst moments, why, why did I make such a stupid mistake?

163
00:10:15,220 --> 00:10:17,620
Why did I go into medicine?

164
00:10:17,620 --> 00:10:20,860
Slowly but surely it lifted.

165
00:10:20,860 --> 00:10:25,100
I began to feel a bit better and, and you know, on a cloudy day there was a crack in

166
00:10:25,100 --> 00:10:29,620
the clouds and the sun started to come out and I wondered, you know, maybe I could feel

167
00:10:29,620 --> 00:10:30,700
better again.

168
00:10:30,700 --> 00:10:39,580
And I made myself a bargain, you know, that if only I redoubled my efforts to be perfect

169
00:10:39,820 --> 00:10:43,180
and never make another mistake again, please make the voices stop.

170
00:10:43,180 --> 00:10:45,380
And they did.

171
00:10:45,380 --> 00:10:50,260
And I went back to work and then it happened again.

172
00:10:50,260 --> 00:10:53,860
Two years later, I was an attending in the emergency department at a community hospital

173
00:10:53,860 --> 00:10:58,420
just north of Toronto and I saw a 25 year old man with a sore throat who was busy.

174
00:10:58,420 --> 00:11:00,020
I was in a bit of a hurry.

175
00:11:00,020 --> 00:11:02,180
He kept pointing here.

176
00:11:02,180 --> 00:11:03,180
I looked at his throat.

177
00:11:03,180 --> 00:11:07,060
It was a little bit pink and I gave him a prescription for penicillin and sent him on

178
00:11:07,060 --> 00:11:08,420
his way.

179
00:11:08,420 --> 00:11:12,860
And even as he was walking out the door, it was still sort of pointing to his throat.

180
00:11:12,860 --> 00:11:17,700
And two days later I came to do my next emergency shift and that's when my chief asked to speak

181
00:11:17,700 --> 00:11:26,140
to me quietly in her office and, and she said the three words, do you remember, do you remember

182
00:11:26,140 --> 00:11:28,340
that patient you saw with the sore throat?

183
00:11:28,340 --> 00:11:31,500
Well, it turns out he didn't have a strep throat.

184
00:11:31,500 --> 00:11:35,780
He had a potentially life threatening condition called epiglottitis.

185
00:11:35,780 --> 00:11:39,660
You can Google it, but it's an infection, not of the throat, but of the upper airway

186
00:11:39,660 --> 00:11:42,900
and it can actually cause the airway to close.

187
00:11:42,900 --> 00:11:45,360
And you know, fortunately he didn't die.

188
00:11:45,360 --> 00:11:51,240
He was placed on intravenous antibiotics and he recovered after a few days.

189
00:11:51,240 --> 00:11:58,140
And I went through the same period of shame and recriminations and felt cleansed and went

190
00:11:58,140 --> 00:12:04,260
back to work until it happened again and again and again.

191
00:12:04,260 --> 00:12:07,740
Twice in one emergency shift I missed appendicitis.

192
00:12:07,740 --> 00:12:12,680
Now that takes some doing, especially when you work in a hospital that at the time saw

193
00:12:12,680 --> 00:12:15,620
about 14 people at night.

194
00:12:15,620 --> 00:12:19,260
Now in both cases, you know, I didn't send them home and I don't think there was any

195
00:12:19,260 --> 00:12:20,260
gap in their care.

196
00:12:20,260 --> 00:12:22,060
One I thought had a kidney stone.

197
00:12:22,060 --> 00:12:24,340
I ordered a kidney x-ray when it turned out to be normal.

198
00:12:24,340 --> 00:12:28,180
My colleague who, who was doing a reassessment of the patient noticed some tenderness in

199
00:12:28,180 --> 00:12:30,280
the right lower quadrant and called the surgeons.

200
00:12:30,280 --> 00:12:31,820
The other one had a lot of diarrhea.

201
00:12:31,820 --> 00:12:38,380
I ordered some fluids to rehydrate him and asked my colleague to reassess him and he

202
00:12:38,380 --> 00:12:39,380
did.

203
00:12:39,380 --> 00:12:42,060
And when he noticed some tenderness in the right lower quadrant called the surgeons.

204
00:12:42,060 --> 00:12:47,220
So in both cases they had their operations and they did okay.

205
00:12:47,220 --> 00:12:50,880
But each time they were gnawing at me, eating at me.

206
00:12:50,880 --> 00:12:54,980
And I'd like to be able to say to you that my worst mistakes only happened in the first

207
00:12:54,980 --> 00:13:00,780
five years of practice, as many of my colleagues say, which is total BS.

208
00:13:00,780 --> 00:13:06,780
Some of my doozies have been in the last five years.

209
00:13:06,780 --> 00:13:09,580
Alone, ashamed and unsupported.

210
00:13:09,580 --> 00:13:11,500
Here's the problem.

211
00:13:11,500 --> 00:13:18,780
If I can't come clean and, and, and, and talk about my mistakes, if I can't find the still

212
00:13:18,780 --> 00:13:23,780
small voice that tells me what really happened, how can I share it with my colleagues?

213
00:13:23,780 --> 00:13:30,260
How can I teach them about what I did so that they don't do the same thing?

214
00:13:30,260 --> 00:13:33,940
If I were to walk into a room, like right now I'm telling, I have no idea what you think

215
00:13:33,940 --> 00:13:35,220
of me.

216
00:13:35,220 --> 00:13:38,820
When was the last time you heard about somebody talk about failure after failure after failure?

217
00:13:38,820 --> 00:13:43,460
Oh yeah, you go to a cocktail party and you might hear about some other doctor, but you're

218
00:13:43,460 --> 00:13:46,940
not going to hear somebody talking about their own mistakes.

219
00:13:46,940 --> 00:13:51,140
If I were to walk into a room filled with my colleagues and ask for their support right

220
00:13:51,140 --> 00:13:55,220
now and start to tell what I've just told you right now, I probably wouldn't get through

221
00:13:55,220 --> 00:14:00,100
two of those stories before they would start to get really uncomfortable.

222
00:14:00,100 --> 00:14:06,060
Somebody would crack a joke, they changed the subject and we would move on.

223
00:14:06,060 --> 00:14:10,780
And in fact, if I knew and my colleagues knew that one of my orthopedic colleagues took

224
00:14:10,780 --> 00:14:15,700
off the wrong leg in my hospital, believe me, I'd have trouble making eye contact with

225
00:14:15,700 --> 00:14:17,740
that person.

226
00:14:17,740 --> 00:14:19,140
That's the system that we have.

227
00:14:19,140 --> 00:14:21,860
It's a complete denial of mistakes.

228
00:14:21,860 --> 00:14:28,100
It's a system in which there are two kinds of positions.

229
00:14:28,100 --> 00:14:32,140
Those who make mistakes and those who don't.

230
00:14:32,140 --> 00:14:35,620
Those who can't handle sleep deprivation and those who can.

231
00:14:35,620 --> 00:14:40,020
Those who have lousy outcomes and those who have great outcomes.

232
00:14:40,020 --> 00:14:45,780
And it's almost like an immunological reaction, like the antibodies begin to attack that person

233
00:14:45,780 --> 00:14:51,900
and we have this idea that if we drive the people who make mistakes out of medicine,

234
00:14:51,900 --> 00:14:56,140
what will we be left with but a safe system?

235
00:14:56,180 --> 00:14:59,020
But there are two problems with that.

236
00:14:59,020 --> 00:15:05,220
In my 20 years or so of medical broadcasting and journalism, I've made a personal study

237
00:15:05,220 --> 00:15:09,260
of medical malpractice and medical errors to learn everything I can from one of the

238
00:15:09,260 --> 00:15:14,080
first articles I wrote for the Toronto Star to my show White Coat Black Art.

239
00:15:14,080 --> 00:15:18,740
And what I've learned is that errors are absolutely ubiquitous.

240
00:15:18,740 --> 00:15:25,160
We live in a, we work in a system where errors happen every day, where one in ten medications

241
00:15:25,200 --> 00:15:29,600
are either the wrong medication given in hospital or at the wrong dosage, where hospital-acquired

242
00:15:29,600 --> 00:15:35,320
infections are getting more and more numerous, causing havoc and death.

243
00:15:35,320 --> 00:15:41,320
In this country, as many as 24,000 Canadians die of preventable medical errors.

244
00:15:41,320 --> 00:15:45,280
In the United States, the Institute of Medicine pegged it at 100,000.

245
00:15:45,280 --> 00:15:50,040
In both cases, these are gross underestimates because we really aren't ferreting out the

246
00:15:50,040 --> 00:15:52,880
problem as we should.

247
00:15:52,880 --> 00:16:00,580
And here's the thing, in a hospital system where medical knowledge is doubling every

248
00:16:00,580 --> 00:16:03,400
two or three years, we can't keep up with it.

249
00:16:03,400 --> 00:16:06,480
Sleep deprivation is absolutely pervasive.

250
00:16:06,480 --> 00:16:08,240
We can't get rid of it.

251
00:16:08,240 --> 00:16:12,920
We have our cognitive biases so that I can take a perfect history on a patient with chest

252
00:16:12,920 --> 00:16:17,200
pain, now take the same patient with chest pain, make them moist and garrulous and put

253
00:16:17,200 --> 00:16:21,720
a little bit of alcohol on their breath, and suddenly my history is laced with contempt.

254
00:16:21,760 --> 00:16:23,680
I don't take the same history.

255
00:16:23,680 --> 00:16:24,680
I'm not a robot.

256
00:16:24,680 --> 00:16:28,640
I don't do things the same way each time, and my patients aren't cars.

257
00:16:28,640 --> 00:16:31,600
They don't tell me their symptoms in the same way each time.

258
00:16:31,600 --> 00:16:34,320
Given all of that, mistakes are inevitable.

259
00:16:34,320 --> 00:16:41,800
So if you take the system as I was taught and weed out all the error-prone health professionals,

260
00:16:41,800 --> 00:16:47,000
well, there won't be anybody left.

261
00:16:47,960 --> 00:16:53,400
You know that business about people not wanting to talk about their worst cases?

262
00:16:53,400 --> 00:16:58,240
On my show on White Coat Black Arrow, I made it a habit of saying, here's my worst mistake.

263
00:16:58,240 --> 00:17:03,760
I would say to everybody from paramedics to the chief of cardiac surgery, here's my worst

264
00:17:03,760 --> 00:17:06,480
mistake, blah, blah, blah, blah, blah, what about yours?

265
00:17:06,480 --> 00:17:11,920
And I would point the microphone towards them, and their pupils would dilate, and they would

266
00:17:11,920 --> 00:17:12,920
recoil.

267
00:17:12,960 --> 00:17:17,960
They would look down and swallow hard and start to tell me their stories.

268
00:17:17,960 --> 00:17:19,600
They want to tell their stories.

269
00:17:19,600 --> 00:17:20,960
They want to share their stories.

270
00:17:20,960 --> 00:17:24,720
They want to be able to say, look, don't make the same mistake I did.

271
00:17:24,720 --> 00:17:27,260
What they need is an environment to be able to do that.

272
00:17:27,260 --> 00:17:34,760
What they need is a redefined medical culture, and it starts with one physician at a time.

273
00:17:34,760 --> 00:17:41,240
The redefined physician is human, knows she's human, accepts it, isn't proud of making mistakes,

274
00:17:41,240 --> 00:17:47,480
but strives to learn one thing from what happened that she can teach to somebody else.

275
00:17:47,480 --> 00:17:49,480
She shares her experience with others.

276
00:17:49,480 --> 00:17:54,480
She's supportive when other people talk about their mistakes, and she points out other people's

277
00:17:54,480 --> 00:18:00,960
mistakes, not in a gotcha way, but in a loving, supportive way so that everybody can benefit.

278
00:18:00,960 --> 00:18:06,480
And she works in a culture of medicine that acknowledges that human beings run the system,

279
00:18:06,480 --> 00:18:10,300
and when human beings run the system, they will make mistakes from time to time.

280
00:18:10,300 --> 00:18:20,640
So the system is evolving to create backups that make it easier to detect those mistakes

281
00:18:20,640 --> 00:18:29,080
that humans inevitably make, and also fosters in a loving, supportive way places where everybody

282
00:18:29,080 --> 00:18:33,820
who is observing in the healthcare system can actually point out things that could be

283
00:18:33,820 --> 00:18:38,360
potential mistakes and is rewarded for doing so, and especially people like me when we

284
00:18:38,400 --> 00:18:40,840
do make mistakes, we're rewarded for coming clean.

285
00:18:40,840 --> 00:18:50,760
My name is Brian Goldman. I am a redefined physician. I'm human. I make mistakes. I'm

286
00:18:50,760 --> 00:18:56,600
sorry about that, but I strive to learn one thing that I can pass on to other people.

287
00:18:56,600 --> 00:19:04,040
I still don't know what you think of me, but I can live with that. And let me close with

288
00:19:04,040 --> 00:19:09,200
three words of my own. I do remember.

